<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <!--
  The contribution API service (Phase 3 of Assets/NewContributeStrategy.md). Runs on the
  project owner's AlmaLinux box behind Apache, bound to 127.0.0.1 only, and is reached at /api/.

  It references CRT.Data so the board-reading and merging rules exist exactly once across the
  desktop app (whose Maintainer tab reviews submissions) and this service - that shared-library goal is the whole
  reason CRT.Data was extracted in Phase 1.

  WHAT MUST NOT LAND HERE:
  - No Avalonia. Same rule as CRT.Data, for the same reason: a server has no business depending on
    a desktop UI framework.
  - No business rule inside Program.cs or inside an endpoint body. Endpoints are a thin rim that
    reads the request into plain values, calls a pure decision function under Handlers/, and maps
    the verdict onto a status code. That split is what lets the decisions be unit tested with no
    server, no database and no network - the same reasoning CLAUDE.md gives for Handlers/Geometry/
    in CRT.App, where ~2000 lines of pure logic were once trapped inside a UserControl.

  PUBLISHING: the release workflow publishes src/CRT.App/CRT.App.csproj by name, never the
  solution, so adding this project cannot affect the signed per-RID release builds. It is deployed
  separately and by hand - see INSTALLING.md.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <!-- Same rule as every other project here: no new warning sneaks into Release. -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!--
    The service is published framework-dependent onto a box that has the ASP.NET Core runtime
    installed (INSTALLING.md installs it), so a solution-wide self-contained publish must not try
    to drag this project along for a desktop RID. Same defensive reasoning as the
    SelfContained=false block in CRT.Data.Tests.csproj, which exists because a solution-wide
    publish otherwise fails on a project that cannot be RID-targeted.
    -->
    <SelfContained>false</SelfContained>
    <RootNamespace>CRT.Server</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>CRT.Server</AssemblyName>
    <!--
    THE VERSION. This service is not shipped to users and has no Velopack update identity, so its
    version exists for exactly one purpose: naming which build is deployed. GET /api/health reports
    InformationalVersion (with the SDK's "+<commit>" metadata stripped - that endpoint is public),
    which is how a deployment is identified and how a failed copy is caught.

    IT IS BUMPED BY CLAUDE, NOT BY HAND (project owner's request, 2026-09-26). The owner bumps
    CRT.App themselves (CRT Maintainer was merged into it on 2026-09-29); this one is judged as real SemVer 2.0.0 by whoever
    changes the server - MAJOR when a client that already works can start failing, MINOR for new
    behaviour that breaks nobody, PATCH for a defect fix or anything unobservable.

    TWO EDITS, ALWAYS TOGETHER: the literal below, and a row in VERSION.md. That file holds the
    policy, the judgement calls and the history; read it before bumping. ServerVersionTests fails
    if the two disagree or if the literal is not valid SemVer, and a Stop hook
    (.claude/hooks/server-version-bump.sh) warns when server code moved and this did not.

    AssemblyVersion is a plain four-part number that takes no pre-release suffix, and nothing reads
    it. Do NOT set InformationalVersion from it (an earlier commented-out line did exactly that):
    the two happen to read the same today, so it would look harmless and then silently peg the
    reported version to 1.0.0 for ever, defeating everything described above. They are separate
    fields on purpose - InformationalVersion is the one /api/health reports and the one to bump.
    -->
    <AssemblyVersion>1.0.0</AssemblyVersion>
    <InformationalVersion>1.0.0</InformationalVersion>

  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\CRT.Data\CRT.Data.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
  The test project reaches a few internal seams - ReviewEndpoints.NotAMaintainer, so the 403 a
  non-maintainer gets is proved to EXECUTE with no authentication scheme registered, which is how
  this service runs. The same "internal plus InternalsVisibleTo rather than public" rule CRT.Data
  follows.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="CRT.Server.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
  The migration scripts must travel with the binaries: the runner reads them from a directory
  beside the assembly at startup, and a publish that left them behind would produce a service
  that refuses to start with "the migrations directory does not exist". CopyToPublishDirectory
  is what makes "dotnet publish" carry them, which CopyToOutputDirectory alone does not
  guarantee for a framework-dependent publish.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Update="Migrations\*.sql">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
      <CopyToPublishDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToPublishDirectory>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
  The systemd unit, shipped beside the binaries so INSTALLING.md can say "copy it" rather than
  have it typed in from a document (2026-10-05). It is a FILE so a test can hold it to the code:
  ServerExitCodesTests reads it, and fails when the exit codes and RestartPreventExitStatus=
  disagree - a setting refused at start would otherwise restart the service every five seconds.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Update="crt-server.service">
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
      <CopyToPublishDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToPublishDirectory>
    </None>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Konscious.Security.Cryptography.Argon2" Version="1.3.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="MySqlConnector" Version="2.6.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
